WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.303
Picture a bank on payday.

2
00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:10.969
Deposits pour in, so its program handles them on eight threads at once, like eight tellers at a counter.

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.260
The next morning, someone checks.

4
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:19.128
Every log line says its deposit went through, yet the balance is a few thousand dollars short.

5
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:22.415
The line behind it looks like one step.

6
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:25.806
So here's the question you might be shy to ask.

7
00:00:25.806 --> 00:00:26.751
It's one line.

8
00:00:26.751 --> 00:00:28.776
How can one line be half done?

9
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.931
Let's shrink the bank to the smallest program that can go wrong.

10
00:00:33.931 --> 00:00:38.241
Two threads share one counter, and each adds one to it, a million times.

11
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.225
That should make two million.

12
00:00:40.225 --> 00:00:41.680
Pause, and make a guess.

13
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.051
Here's what the lesson's demo printed, in five runs on a four-core machine.

14
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:53.054
Four wrong answers, and one right one, from the same code.

15
00:00:53.054 --> 00:00:55.339
No crash, no exception, no warning.

16
00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:58.581
If it were wrong every time, at least we would notice.

17
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.813
That fifth run is the sneaky one.

18
00:01:00.813 --> 00:01:05.939
Sometimes the threads don't collide, and the broken program prints the right answer.

19
00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:14.544
Shrink the loop to a thousand each, and every run prints exactly 2,000, because the first thread finishes before the second starts.

20
00:01:14.544 --> 00:01:16.335
That's why small tests pass.

21
00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:20.752
To see why, go back to a bank before computers.

22
00:01:20.752 --> 00:01:22.785
The balance lives on one card.

23
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.428
To add a deposit, a teller reads the card, works out the new number on scratch paper, and writes it back.

24
00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:30.620
Let's map it.

25
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.441
Each teller is a thread.

26
00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:36.236
The card is the field in memory that every thread shares.

27
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:42.575
The scratch paper is the teller's own, like a register, a tiny storage slot inside the core.

28
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.583
And read, add, write is exactly what count++ does.

29
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.971
The JVM's own instructions agree.

30
00:01:49.971 --> 00:01:55.201
Bytecode is the list of simple steps the Java compiler turns your code into.

31
00:01:55.433 --> 00:02:01.072
For count++, it says: read count, load the number one, add, and write count back.

32
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.001
Now two tellers, one card, at 41.

33
00:02:05.233 --> 00:02:07.117
Teller one reads 41.

34
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:10.753
Before she writes, teller two reads it too: 41.

35
00:02:10.967 --> 00:02:13.683
Teller one adds one, and writes 42.

36
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.322
Teller two adds one.

37
00:02:15.322 --> 00:02:17.241
What does the card say now?

38
00:02:19.867 --> 00:02:20.144
42.

39
00:02:20.144 --> 00:02:23.848
Two deposits, and the card moved by one.

40
00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:25.673
That's a lost update.

41
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.084
Both tellers did every step right.

42
00:02:28.084 --> 00:02:30.460
Each would pass a performance review.

43
00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:31.680
The bank would not.

44
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.744
The mistake lives in the gap between the steps.

45
00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:37.596
This pattern is read-modify-write.

46
00:02:37.596 --> 00:02:42.669
And a bug where the result depends on how threads take turns is a race condition.

47
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.879
So how can one line be half done?

48
00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:49.975
To the machine, it's three steps, and another thread can run between any two of them.

49
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.379
Back to the bank.

50
00:02:52.379 --> 00:03:00.493
The usual next move is a thread-safe map, one that's safe to use from many threads, like the JDK's ConcurrentHashMap.

51
00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:06.591
The lesson's payday demo has eight tellers each make 200,000 one-cent deposits.

52
00:03:06.591 --> 00:03:07.709
Three versions.

53
00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:09.723
Guess which come out exact.

54
00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:15.333
The plain field lost more than two thirds.

55
00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:17.667
The thread-safe map lost even more.

56
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.958
Every single call on it is safe.

57
00:03:19.958 --> 00:03:25.103
But get, and then put, is two calls, and the gap between them belongs to nobody.

58
00:03:25.333 --> 00:03:33.191
merge does the read, the add and the write as one step on that key, like a teller who keeps a hand on the card from read to write.

59
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.969
A step that no other thread can split is called atomic.

60
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.725
The second shape of race hides in an if.

61
00:03:40.725 --> 00:03:45.458
A customer taps Pay on a slow network, and the app sends the payment again.

62
00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.415
Two tellers get the two copies.

63
00:03:47.633 --> 00:03:50.613
Each checks the book: recorded yet?

64
00:03:50.613 --> 00:03:50.868
No.

65
00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.733
Each does a slow check that it's genuine, and each records it.

66
00:03:54.967 --> 00:04:03.272
In the lesson's demo, eight tellers got the same 1,000 payments, and one run recorded them 2,579 times.

67
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.498
Each customer paid once.

68
00:04:05.498 --> 00:04:10.575
The shops were paid 2,579 times, and nobody there complained.

69
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.219
That's check-then-act.

70
00:04:12.219 --> 00:04:15.123
A check is out of date the moment it returns.

71
00:04:15.333 --> 00:04:18.472
The fix is one call that checks and acts together.

72
00:04:18.472 --> 00:04:22.237
putIfAbsent: claim this payment, unless someone already has.

73
00:04:22.467 --> 00:04:25.037
Only one teller can win the claim.

74
00:04:25.037 --> 00:04:27.003
Now it's 1,000, every run.

75
00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:31.376
Here are both fixes, in Java and in Kotlin.

76
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.892
A thread-safe class makes each call atomic, never two calls in a row.

77
00:04:35.892 --> 00:04:38.256
Test with threads that really collide.

78
00:04:38.256 --> 00:04:44.228
And one call protects one key: moving money between two accounts needs a lock, which comes next.

79
00:04:45.433 --> 00:04:51.985
So, count++ is a read, an add and a write, and the gaps belong to whoever gets there first.

80
00:04:51.985 --> 00:04:54.001
Make the whole job one step.

81
00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:55.897
Quick check.

82
00:04:55.897 --> 00:04:59.400
If the balance is at least the amount, take the amount.

83
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.374
Two withdrawals run it at once.

84
00:05:01.374 --> 00:05:02.520
What can go wrong?

85
00:05:05.533 --> 00:05:10.073
Both see enough money, both take it, and the account goes below zero.

86
00:05:10.073 --> 00:05:11.520
Check-then-act, again.
